{{!-- Board view of the relationship hearts: the same rows the table renders, dealt into
      three standing lanes (Hostile / Neutral / Trusted, coldest first so the board runs the
      same direction as the rating). Hostile and Trusted each split into two exact-value
      ZONES, so a drop can name a rating rather than just a band: 1|2 and 4|5. Neutral holds
      only its own value and is not split.

      A lane is a READING of the 1-5 rating, not a stored field, so toggling back to the
      table shows identical data.

      Params: lanes (from buildRelationshipLanes), canRate, canToggle, showTooltip.

      `{{#with . as |board|}}` aliases the partial's own params so the three nested {{#each}}
      levels can reach them by NAME instead of counting `../` hops. Block params are
      lexically scoped, so nesting this markup deeper later cannot silently repoint a
      reference at the wrong context — which is exactly the bug that positional lookups
      invite.

      Each card keeps the live note field and show/hide box, reusing the very same
      .stonetop-rel-note-input / .stonetop-rel-show-check hooks the table uses —
      wireRelationshipTable's delegated handlers drive them here with no new write path.
      The note is a textarea rather than the table's input so a long one can be opened and
      read in full (see the field itself, and wireNoteExpanders), but it saves identically.

      The table's five-slot .stonetop-rel-heart strip does NOT come across: a card shows a
      single .stonetop-rel-heart-badge instead, which is display-only (aria-hidden, matched by
      no handler) because five 18px slots ate a whole row of a ~180px card. Ratings are set on
      the board by the card's step buttons, the arrow keys, or a drop onto a zone. --}}
<div class="stonetop-rel-board">
	{{!-- A card moving column is invisible to a screen reader, so every move is spoken. --}}
	<div class="stonetop-rel-board-live" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"></div>

	{{#with . as |board|}}
	{{#each lanes as |lane|}}
	{{!-- No --split modifier class: whether a lane offers a value choice is expressed by
	      whether it renders a drop overlay at all, so a parallel class hook would be a second
	      source of truth for the same fact. --}}
	<section class="stonetop-rel-lane stonetop-rel-lane--{{lane.key}}" data-rel-lane="{{lane.key}}">
		<header class="stonetop-rel-lane-head">
			<i class="{{lane.icon}}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
			<span class="stonetop-rel-lane-title">{{localize lane.labelKey}}</span>
			<span class="stonetop-rel-lane-count" aria-hidden="true">{{lane.count}}</span>
		</header>
		<p class="stonetop-rel-lane-hint">{{localize lane.hintKey}}</p>

		{{!-- Positioning context for the drop overlay, which covers exactly the card area. --}}
		<div class="stonetop-rel-lane-body">
			{{#if lane.isSplit}}
			{{!-- Exact-value drop zones, shown ONLY while a card is being dragged (the board
			      gets .is-dragging) and inert the rest of the time. An overlay rather than real
			      sub-columns because permanently splitting a ~185px lane would cost every card
			      half its width to serve an occasional gesture; a dropped card fills the lane.

			      data-rel-zone is the exact rating a drop here asserts, read straight off this
			      element, so the number aimed at and the number written are the same one.

			      aria-hidden: this is a pointer affordance with no keyboard role. The keyboard
			      reaches all five ratings with the arrow keys instead, one per press. --}}
			<div class="stonetop-rel-dropzones" aria-hidden="true">
				{{#each lane.zones as |zone|}}
				<div class="stonetop-rel-zone" data-rel-zone="{{zone.hearts}}">
					<span class="stonetop-rel-zone-label">{{localize zone.labelKey}}</span>
					<span class="stonetop-rel-zone-value">{{zone.hearts}}</span>
				</div>
				{{/each}}
			</div>
			{{/if}}

			<ul class="stonetop-rel-lane-cards" aria-label="{{localize lane.labelKey}}">
				{{#each lane.cards}}
				{{!-- data-rel-hearts / data-rel-rated are what the drag, click and keyboard
				      handlers read back, so all three agree with exactly what was rendered.

				      tabindex only when the viewer can rate: the arrow-key handler is wired
				      behind that same flag, so a focusable card would otherwise be a tab stop
				      that does nothing. Read-only, a card is a plain list item.

				      An unrated card is dimmed and dashed and says so nowhere else: the
				      "never rated" wording is gone from the tooltip and the accessible name
				      alike, so the card reads as plainly as any other and the treatment is
				      the whole of the hint. data-rel-rated still carries the fact for the
				      handlers, which is what the dashed styling keys off.

				      data-rel-index is where the card SITS in this lane and data-rel-order is
				      the position someone stored for it, and the two are genuinely different
				      questions: the index is what a reorder counts from, while the order is
				      empty on a card nobody has placed — which is exactly the difference
				      between "third in the column" and "pinned third". The reorder patch needs
				      both, to move from the one and to write only the rows whose stored number
				      actually changes. An unplaced card renders an EMPTY order, never a 0,
				      which is a real position. --}}
				<li class="stonetop-rel-card{{#unless rated}} stonetop-rel-card--unrated{{/unless}}{{#unless shown}} stonetop-rel-card--hidden{{/unless}}"
				    data-rel-id="{{this.id}}" data-rel-hearts="{{this.hearts}}" data-rel-name="{{this.name}}"
				    data-rel-rated="{{#if rated}}true{{else}}false{{/if}}"
				    data-rel-index="{{this.laneIndex}}" data-rel-order="{{this.order}}"
				    {{#if board.canRate}}tabindex="0"{{/if}}
				    aria-label="{{this.feeling}}">
					<div class="stonetop-rel-card-head">
						{{#if board.canToggle}}
						<input type="checkbox" class="stonetop-rel-show-check" data-rel-id="{{this.id}}"
						       {{#if shown}}checked{{/if}}
						       data-tooltip='{{#if board.showTooltip}}{{board.showTooltip}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.relationships.showTooltip"}}{{/if}}'
						       aria-label='{{#if board.showTooltip}}{{board.showTooltip}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.relationships.showTooltip"}}{{/if}}'>
						{{/if}}
						{{#if img}}
						{{!-- draggable="false" so a press on the face drags the CARD, not the image.
						      data-name is the hover preview's caption and its selector hook — see
						      wireRelationshipLinks. --}}
						{{!-- .stonetop-rel-portrait is the CLIPPING BOX and appears exactly once
						      per card: the glyph span below already IS the box. --}}
						<span class="stonetop-rel-portrait stonetop-portrait-box"><img class="stonetop-rel-portrait-img" src="{{this.img}}" alt="" draggable="false" data-name="{{this.name}}"{{#if imgStyle}} style="{{this.imgStyle}}"{{/if}}></span>
						{{else}}
						{{!-- A settlement wears its own icon; only a person gets the silhouette.
						      No image, so no hover preview. --}}
						<span class="stonetop-rel-portrait stonetop-rel-portrait--empty"><i class="{{#if icon}}{{this.icon}}{{else}}fas fa-user{{/if}}"></i></span>
						{{/if}}
						{{!-- Actor-backed names open their sheet. wireLaneDrag already refuses to arm
						      on an `a`, so the link is a click target inside a draggable card without
						      the two contending; a settlement has no uuid and stays a plain span.
						      A second tab stop inside the card is deliberate: with no href the
						      browser gives the link neither focus nor Enter, and the card's own
						      tabindex only reaches the card. The card's arrow keys are unaffected —
						      they are bound in the bubble phase off the CARD, and this is a
						      different focus target.

						      aria-label, not data-tooltip: with no href, nothing about this
						      element says it goes anywhere, and a tab stop that announces only a
						      name gives a screen-reader user nothing to act on. No hover bubble,
						      because the cards are packed tight enough that one would cover the
						      neighbours — the same call the note caret makes. --}}
						{{#if uuid}}
						<a class="stonetop-rel-card-name stonetop-rel-open" data-actor-uuid="{{this.uuid}}" data-actor-id="{{this.id}}"
						   role="link" tabindex="0"
						   aria-label='{{localize "stonetop.relationships.open" name=name}}'>{{this.name}}</a>
						{{else}}
						<span class="stonetop-rel-card-name" data-tooltip-text="{{#if tooltip}}{{this.tooltip}}{{else}}{{this.name}}{{/if}}">{{this.name}}</span>
						{{/if}}
						{{!-- One heart with the rating inside it, at the far right of the name's line,
						      instead of the table's five-slot strip. Five 18px slots ate a whole row
						      of a card that is only ~180px wide, for a value a single glyph can carry.

						      Display only, unlike the table's strip: aria-hidden because the card's own
						      aria-label already reads the full sentence ("Vera loves Aldric"), so
						      exposing the numeral too would say it twice. Exact values are set by
						      dragging onto a zone or by the arrow keys. --}}
						<span class="stonetop-rel-heart-badge" data-tooltip-text="{{this.feeling}}" aria-hidden="true">
							<span class="stonetop-rel-heart-badge-value">{{this.hearts}}</span>
						</span>
					</div>

					{{#if board.canRate}}
					{{!-- Move the card one sub-column left, home to Neutral, or one sub-column
					      right. The outer two carry `dir` and name an EXACT rating (data-rel-step),
					      so between them they reach all five values one press at a time; the middle
					      one names the Neutral band, which holds a single value anyway.

					      The old strip was three band shortcuts (Hostile / Neutral / Trusted), which
					      stopped making sense once a lane held two ratings: a band button wrote only
					      its gentle end, so pressing "Hostile" on a card already in Hostile did
					      nothing, and nothing in the strip could tell a 4 from a 5.

					      A step at the end of the scale renders truly disabled, not merely dimmed:
					      it stays in place so the Neutral segment never shifts under the pointer,
					      and the browser refuses the press outright rather than accepting a click
					      that quietly does nothing — which is the exact confusion the band buttons
					      caused. No data-rel-step on it either, so the handler has nothing to read.

					      No --step modifier class: `dir` is already on the element as data-rel-dir,
					      and CSS can select that, so a parallel class hook would be a second source
					      of truth for the same fact.

					      Every label formats through one expression: {target} is the rating the press
					      would write, or — at a dead end — the one the card already holds. Neutral's
					      label has no placeholder and simply ignores it. --}}
					{{!-- Standing only. Position is NOT a button: a card is arranged within its
					      lane by dragging it, or with alt+up/down on a focused card, and there is
					      deliberately no up/down strip beside this one — four arrows on a ~180px
					      card read as one control, and two of them meant something entirely
					      different from the other two. --}}
					<div class="stonetop-segmented-picker stonetop-rel-lane-picker" role="group"
					     aria-label='{{localize "stonetop.relationships.lane.pickerLabel"}}'>
						{{#each moveButtons}}
						<button type="button"
						        class="stonetop-segmented-picker-option stonetop-rel-lane-btn{{#if isCurrent}} is-active{{/if}}"
						        {{#if dir}}
						        data-rel-dir="{{this.dir}}"{{#if disabled}} disabled="disabled"{{else}} data-rel-step="{{this.hearts}}"{{/if}}
						        {{else}}
						        data-rel-lane="{{this.laneKey}}" aria-pressed="{{#if isCurrent}}true{{else}}false{{/if}}"
						        {{/if}}
						        data-tooltip="{{localize labelKey target=(localize targetLabelKey)}}"
						        aria-label="{{localize labelKey target=(localize targetLabelKey)}}">
							<i class="{{this.icon}}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
						</button>
						{{/each}}
					</div>
					{{/if}}

					{{!-- NOT .steading-resident-input: the steading sheet delegates on that class
					      and stopPropagation()s its change event.

					      A <textarea>, not the table's <input>, and the one place the two views'
					      markup genuinely diverges. A card is ~180px wide, so most notes are longer
					      than the line they are clipped to — and a clipped <input> cannot be read at
					      all here: it does not wrap, it carries no tooltip, and unlike the table's
					      Notes column there is no border to drag wider. Opening the note grows it to
					      fit every line it holds; collapsed it is dressed to be the same one-line
					      field the table shows.

					      Still `.stonetop-rel-note-input`, so wireRelationshipTable's delegated
					      change handler saves it with no new write path — a textarea fires `change`
					      on blur exactly as the input does. Enter is blocked in wireNoteExpanders:
					      the same note is edited through an <input> over in the table view, and an
					      <input> strips CR/LF out of its own value, so a line break typed here would
					      vanish the first time someone touched the note there. --}}
					<div class="stonetop-rel-card-note-wrap">
						<textarea class="stonetop-rel-note-input stonetop-rel-card-note" data-rel-id="{{this.id}}"
						          rows="1" placeholder='{{localize "stonetop.relationships.notePlaceholder"}}'
						          {{#unless board.canRate}}disabled{{/unless}}>{{this.notes}}</textarea>
						{{!-- The collapsed note's visible text, and the ONLY reason it exists is the
						      ellipsis: `text-overflow` is inert on a <textarea> in both Chromium and
						      Gecko, so a clipped note ends mid-word with nothing to say it was cut.
						      A span is a plain text box, so the browser ellipsises it — and it does so
						      by its own layout, which is the whole point: the `is-overflowing` class
						      this replaces needed a JS measure per render plus a ResizeObserver, and
						      read 0 for everything on a board rendered inside an inactive tab. Nothing
						      is measured here, so nothing can be measured wrong.

						      Laid exactly over the field and pointer-transparent, with the textarea's
						      own text turned transparent underneath while collapsed — so a click still
						      lands on the field, focuses it, and opens the note, at which point this
						      hides and the real text comes back. The placeholder is unaffected (it
						      carries its own colour), so an empty note reads as it always did.

						      aria-hidden: the textarea below still holds the same string and is what a
						      screen reader announces. Without this the note would be read twice. --}}
						<span class="stonetop-rel-note-clip" aria-hidden="true">{{this.notes}}</span>
						{{!-- On every card, and shown on every card — including one whose note is
						      short enough to fit its line, or empty. A control that appeared only
						      where there was something hidden had to be re-found on each card, and
						      it depended on a per-render measurement that read 0 on a board rendered
						      inside an inactive tab. Emitted regardless of canRate: reading
						      someone's note is not an edit, and a viewer who cannot type in the
						      field is exactly the one who has no other way to see the end of it.
						      aria-expanded and the label are kept in step by the same handler. Labelled
						      but deliberately NOT given a data-tooltip: the caret sits directly under the
						      note it opens, so a hover bubble lands on top of the text the user is trying
						      to read. --}}
						<button type="button" class="stonetop-rel-note-toggle" aria-expanded="false"
						        aria-label='{{localize "stonetop.relationships.noteExpand"}}'>
							{{!-- A masked note-caret.svg rather than fa-chevron-down: the control is a wide,
							      shallow lid under the note, and the FA glyph only reached those proportions
							      by being scaled on one axis, which thinned its stroke and threw off the
							      vertical centring the font builds into the glyph. --}}
							<span class="stonetop-rel-note-caret" aria-hidden="true"></span>
						</button>
					</div>
				</li>
				{{/each}}

				{{#if lane.isEmpty}}
				<li class="stonetop-rel-lane-empty">{{localize "stonetop.relationships.lane.empty"}}</li>
				{{/if}}
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>
	{{/each}}
	{{/with}}
</div>
